// Shared, framework-neutral message for the HIL 3-way comparison, at payload shape P3
// (examples/perf_hil/OPTIMIZATION_PLAN.md section 3). One of four: idl/p1..p4/Bench.idl.
//
// All four are named Bench.idl and declare the same `struct Bench`, so every scenario's own
// client/server compiles unchanged at each size and the size is chosen purely by which directory
// the generator is pointed at (build.sh's second argument). Four differently-named structs would
// have put a type switch into every harness source, and the harness would then no longer be the
// same code at the four sizes - which is what this comparison rests on.
//
// Both fastddsgen and CycloneDDS's own idlc compile this exact file - "identical data" is a
// byte-identical source guarantee, not a hand-matched one (COMPARISON.MD's own design note). The
// TickLE side is generated too, since 2026-09-25, from the matching
// tickle/common/p3/Bench.msg; tools/scripts/check_bench_shapes.sh checks the two against each
// other so this file and that one cannot drift apart unnoticed.
//
// Field order is `send_ns` before `seq` deliberately, and must stay that way. Standard CDR aligns
// a 64-bit field to 8 while TickLE's CDR-4 caps alignment at 4, so with `seq` first this file
// produced 1424 bytes on TickLE's wire and four more on both vendors' - a difference that would
// have landed inside the wire-bytes-per-sample metric and been charged to framing. `send_ns`
// first is 8 + 4 + N under both rules, with no padding under either.
//
// 8 + 4 + 1412 = 1424 bytes: deliberately asymmetric. It fits one TickLE frame and forces both
// vendors to split. The window is narrow and measured - one frame holds a sample up to 1443.4 on
// TickLE and 1409.9 on CycloneDDS - so this size has to sit between them; 1424 leaves 19.4 bytes
// below one edge and 14.1 above the other, and check_bench_shapes.sh gates both. **A "TickLE wins
// at large messages" claim must not be read off this size** - under loss a two-fragment datagram is lost
// ~9.75% of the time against 5% for one, so TickLE would show half the effective loss from the
// size alone. P2 is the like-for-like large size; this one measures the cost of the boundary.
//
// @extensibility(FINAL) pinned explicitly, not left to whatever each tool's own current default
// happens to be (idlc warns this may change to "appendable" in a future release) - both vendors
// must interpret this identically regardless of tool version.
@extensibility(FINAL)
struct Bench {
    unsigned long long send_ns; // sender's own monotonic send timestamp (CLOCK_MONOTONIC, ns)
    unsigned long seq;          // per-Publisher monotonic sample counter, starts at 1
    octet payload[1412];          // fixed filler, unused by any scenario's own logic
};
